# Managed by dotfiles. Do not edit in place -- edit ssh/config in the repo.
#
# SSH keys live in the Secure Enclave via Secretive, so the private half never
# exists as a file on disk. It cannot be exported, copied, or read by anything
# running on this machine, and every signature needs Touch ID.
#
# ControlMaster keeps one authenticated connection to GitHub alive in the
# background, so a work session costs a single Touch ID rather than one per
# push. Note ControlPersist is an IDLE timer: it resets on every use, so a
# connection you keep using outlives the 4h. To close it on demand:
#
#     ssh -O exit git@github.com
#
# The Match block below deliberately has no host criterion, so Secretive's agent
# is used for EVERY host, not just GitHub. The SSH_AUTH_SOCK export in
# ~/.zshrc.custom already covers anything launched from a shell; this is what
# covers GUI apps (VS Code, editors, git clients) that do not inherit the shell
# environment and would otherwise find no agent at all.
#
# It is guarded on the socket actually existing, so this file stays valid before
# Secretive has been set up and on machines without it -- ssh just falls back to
# whatever SSH_AUTH_SOCK points at. Cost is one `test -S` fork per ssh call.

Match exec "test -S %d/Library/Containers/com.maxgoedjen.Secretive.SecretAgent/Data/socket.ssh"
  IdentityAgent ~/Library/Containers/com.maxgoedjen.Secretive.SecretAgent/Data/socket.ssh

Host github.com
  User git
  ControlMaster auto
  ControlPath ~/.ssh/cm-%C
  ControlPersist 4h

# Once you keep more than a couple of keys in the agent, pin the right one to
# avoid "Too many authentication failures":
#   IdentityFile ~/.ssh/<key>.pub
#   IdentitiesOnly yes
